    nullprogram.com/blog/2024/02/05/
  </div>

  <p>If you=E2=80=99ve spent much time developing with Mingw-w64 you=E2=80=
=99ve likely seen the
symbol <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">___chkstk_ms</c=
ode>, perhaps in an error message. It=E2=80=99s a little piece of
runtime provided by GCC via libgcc which ensures enough of the stack is
committed for the caller=E2=80=99s stack frame. The =E2=80=9Cfunction=E2=80=
=9D uses a custom ABI
and is implemented in assembly. So is the subject of this article, a
slightly improved implementation soon to be included in <a href=3D"https://=
nullprogram.com/blog/2020/05/15/">w64devkit</a> as
libchkstk (<code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">-lchkstk</c=
ode>).</p>

<p>The MSVC toolchain has an identical (x64) or similar (x86) function name=
d
<code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">__chkstk</code>. We=E2=
=80=99ll discuss that as well, and w64devkit will include x86 and
x64 implementations, useful when linking with MSVC object files. The new
x86 <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">__chkstk</code> in=
 particular is also better than the MSVC definition.</p>

<p>A note on spelling: <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge"=
>___chkstk_ms</code> is spelled with three underscores, and
<code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">__chkstk</code> is spe=
lled with two. On x86, <a href=3D"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/cpp/bui=
ld/reference/decorated-names#FormatC"><code class=3D"language-plaintext hig=
hlighter-rouge">cdecl</code> functions</a> are
decorated with a leading underscore, and so may be rendered, e.g. in error
messages, with one fewer underscore. The true name is undecorated, and the
raw symbol name is identical on x86 and x64. Further complicating matters,
libgcc defines a <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">___ch=
kstk</code> with three underscores. As far as I can tell,
this spelling arose from confusion regarding name decoration, but nobody=E2=
=80=99s
noticed for the past 28 years. libgcc=E2=80=99s x64 <code class=3D"language=
-plaintext highlighter-rouge">___chkstk</code> is obviously and
badly broken, so I=E2=80=99m sure nobody has ever used it anyway, not even =
by
accident thanks to the misspelling. I=E2=80=99ll touch on that below.</p>

<p>When referring to a particular instance, I will use a specific spelling.
Otherwise the term =E2=80=9Cchkstk=E2=80=9D refers to the family. If you=E2=
=80=99d like to skip
ahead to the source for libchkstk: <strong><a href=3D"https://github.com/sk=
eeto/w64devkit/blob/master/src/libchkstk.S"><code class=3D"language-plainte=
xt highlighter-rouge">libchkstk.S</code></a></strong>.</p>

<h3 id=3D"a-gradually-committed-stack">A gradually committed stack</h3>

<p>The header of a Windows executable lists two stack sizes: a <em>reserve<=
/em> size
and an initial <em>commit</em> size. The first is the largest the main thre=
ad
stack can grow, and the second is the amount <a href=3D"https://learn.micro=
soft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/memoryapi/nf-memoryapi-virtualalloc">commi=
tted</a> when the
program starts. A program gradually commits stack pages <em>as needed</em> =
up to
the reserve size. Binutils <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-ro=
uge">objdump</code> option <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-ro=
uge">-p</code> lists the sizes. Typical
output for a Mingw-w64 program:</p>

<div class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight=
"><pre class=3D"highlight"><code>$ objdump -p example.exe | grep SizeOfStac=
k
SizeOfStackReserve      0000000000200000
SizeOfStackCommit       0000000000001000
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>The values are in hexadecimal, and this indicates 2MiB reserved and 4KiB
initially committed. With the Binutils linker, <code class=3D"language-plai=
ntext highlighter-rouge">ld</code>, you can set them at
link time using <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">--stac=
k</code>. Via <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">gcc</cod=
e>, use <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">-Xlinker</code=
>. For example, to
reserve an 8MiB stack and commit half of it:</p>

<div class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight=
"><pre class=3D"highlight"><code>$ gcc -Xlinker --stack=3D$((8&lt;&lt;20)),=
$((4&lt;&lt;20)) ...
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>MSVC <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">link.exe</code=
> similarly has <a href=3D"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/cpp/build/refe=
rence/stack-stack-allocations"><code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighte=
r-rouge">/stack</code></a>.</p>

<p>The purpose of this mechanism is to avoid paying the <em>commit charge</=
em> for
unused stack. It made sense 30 years ago when stacks were a potentially
large portion of physical memory. These days it=E2=80=99s a rounding error =
and
silly we=E2=80=99re still dealing with it. Using the above options you can =
choose
to commit the entire stack up front, at which point a chkstk helper is no
longer needed (<a href=3D"https://gcc.gnu.org/bugzilla/show_bug.cgi?id=3D59=
532"><code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">-mno-stack-arg-pr=
obe</code></a>, <a href=3D"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/cpp/build/refe=
rence/gs-control-stack-checking-calls"><code class=3D"language-plaintext hi=
ghlighter-rouge">/Gs2147483647</code></a>). This
requires link-time control of the main module, which isn=E2=80=99t always a=
n
option, like when supplying a DLL for someone else to run.</p>

<p>The program grows the stack by touching the singular <a href=3D"https://=
devblogs.microsoft.com/oldnewthing/20220203-00/?p=3D106215">guard page</a>
mapped between the committed and uncommitted portions of the stack. This
action triggers a page fault, and the default fault handler commits the
guard page and maps a new guard page just below. In other words, the stack
grows one page at a time, in order.</p>

<p>In most cases nothing special needs to happen. The guard page mechanism =
is
transparent and in the background. However, if a function stack frame
exceeds the page size then there=E2=80=99s a chance that it might leap over=
 the
guard page, crashing the program. To prevent this, compilers insert a
chkstk call in the function prologue. Before local variable allocation,
chkstk walks down the stack =E2=80=94 that is, towards lower addresses =E2=
=80=94 nudging
the guard page with each step. (As a side effect it provides <a href=3D"htt=
ps://nullprogram.com/blog/2017/06/21/">stack clash
protection</a> =E2=80=94 the only security aspect of chkstk.) For example:<=
/p>

<div class=3D"language-c highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight"><pre c=
lass=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"kt">void</span> <span class=3D"nf">=
callee</span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"kt">char</span> <span=
 class=3D"o">*</span><span class=3D"p">);</span>

<span class=3D"kt">void</span> <span class=3D"nf">example</span><span class=
=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"kt">void</span><span class=3D"p">)</span>
<span class=3D"p">{</span>
    <span class=3D"kt">char</span> <span class=3D"n">large</span><span clas=
s=3D"p">[</span><span class=3D"mi">1</span><span class=3D"o">&lt;&lt;</span=
><span class=3D"mi">20</span><span class=3D"p">];</span>
    <span class=3D"n">callee</span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D=
"n">large</span><span class=3D"p">);</span>
<span class=3D"p">}</span>
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>Compiled with 64-bit <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge=
">gcc -O</code>:</p>

<div class=3D"language-nasm highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight"><pr=
e class=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"nl">example:</span>
    <span class=3D"nf">movl</span>    <span class=3D"kc">$</span><span clas=
s=3D"mi">1048616</span><span class=3D"p">,</span> <span class=3D"o">%</span=
