<p>Each call to these functions with such large local arrays is also a call
to chkstk. Though with a 64KiB frame, that=E2=80=99s only 16 iterations; ba=
rely
detectable in a benchmark. If the function touches the file system, which
is likely when processing paths, then chkstk doesn=E2=80=99t matter at all.=
 My
starting example had a 1MiB array, or 256 chkstk iterations. That starts
to become measurable, though it=E2=80=99s also pushing the limits. At that =
point
you <a href=3D"https://nullprogram.com/blog/2023/09/27/">ought to be using =
a scratch arena</a>.</p>

<p>So ultimately after writing an improved <code class=3D"language-plaintex=
t highlighter-rouge">___chkstk_ms</code> I could only
measure a tiny difference in contrived programs, and none in any real
application. Though there=E2=80=99s still one more benefit I haven=E2=80=99=
t yet
mentioned=E2=80=A6</p>

<h3 id=3D"the-first-thing-we-do-lets-kill-all-the-lawyers">=E2=80=9CThe fir=
st thing we do, let=E2=80=99s <a href=3D"https://nullprogram.com/blog/2023/=
06/22/#119-henry-vi">kill all the lawyers</a>=E2=80=9D.</h3>

<p>My original motivation for this project wasn=E2=80=99t the optimization =
=E2=80=94 which I
didn=E2=80=99t even discover until after I had started =E2=80=94 but <em>li=
censing</em>. I hate
software licenses, and the <a href=3D"https://nullprogram.com/blog/2023/01/=
18/">tools I=E2=80=99ve written for w64devkit</a>
are dedicated to the public domain. Both source <em>and</em> binaries (as
distributed). I can do so because <a href=3D"https://nullprogram.com/blog/2=
023/02/15/">I don=E2=80=99t link runtime components</a>,
not even libgcc. Not <a href=3D"https://nullprogram.com/blog/2023/05/31/">e=
ven header files</a>. Every byte of code in those
binaries is my work or the work of my collaborators.</p>

<p>Every once in awhile <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge=
">___chkstk_ms</code> rears its ugly head, and I have to
make a decision. Do I re-work my code to avoid it? Do I take the reigns of
the linker and disable stack probes? I haven=E2=80=99t necessarily allocate=
d a
large local array: A bit of luck with function inlining can combine
several smaller stack frames into one that=E2=80=99s just large enough to r=
equire
chkstk.</p>

<p>Since libgcc falls under the <a href=3D"https://www.gnu.org/licenses/gcc=
-exception-3.1.html">GCC Runtime Library Exception</a>, if it=E2=80=99s
linked into my program through an =E2=80=9CEligible Compilation Process=E2=
=80=9D =E2=80=94 which I
believe includes w64devkit =E2=80=94 then the GPL-licensed functions embedd=
ed in
my binary are legally siloed and the GPL doesn=E2=80=99t infect the rest of=
 the
program. These bits are still GPL in isolation, and if someone were to
copy them out of the program then they=E2=80=99d be normal GPL code again. =
In
other words, it=E2=80=99s not a 100% public domain binary if libgcc was lin=
ked!</p>

<p>(If some FSF lawyer says I=E2=80=99m wrong, then this is an escape hatch=
 through
which anyone can scrub the GPL from GCC runtime code, and then ignore the
runtime exception entirely.)</p>

<p>MSVC is worse. Hardly anyone follows its license, but fortunately for mo=
st
the license is practically unenforced. Its chkstk, which currently resides
in a loose <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">chkstk.obj<=
/code>, falls into what Microsoft calls =E2=80=9CDistributable
Code.=E2=80=9D Its license requires =E2=80=9Cexternal end users to agree to=
 terms that
protect the Distributable Code.=E2=80=9D In other words, if you compile a p=
rogram
with MSVC, you=E2=80=99re required to have a EULA including the relevant te=
rms
from the Visual Studio license. You=E2=80=99re not legally permitted to dis=
tribute
software in the manner of w64devkit =E2=80=94 no installer, just a portable=
 zip
distribution =E2=80=94 if that software has been built with MSVC.  At least=
 not
without special care which nobody does. (Don=E2=80=99t worry, I won=E2=80=
=99t tell.)</p>

<h3 id=3D"how-to-use-libchkstk">How to use libchkstk</h3>

<p>To avoid libgcc entirely you need <code class=3D"language-plaintext high=
lighter-rouge">-nostdlib</code>. Otherwise it=E2=80=99s implicitly
offered to the linker, and you=E2=80=99d need to manually check if it picke=
d up
code from libgcc. If <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">l=
d</code> complains about a missing chkstk, use <code class=3D"language-plai=
ntext highlighter-rouge">-lchkstk</code>
to get a definition. If you use <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlight=
er-rouge">-lchkstk</code> when it=E2=80=99s not needed, nothing
happens, so it=E2=80=99s safe to always include.</p>

<p>I also recently added <a href=3D"https://github.com/skeeto/w64devkit/blo=
b/master/src/libmemory.c">a libmemory</a> to w64devkit, providing tiny,
public domain definitions of <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-=
rouge">memset</code>, <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">=
memcpy</code>, <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">memmove=
</code>, <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">memcmp</code>=
, and
<code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">strlen</code>. All com=
pilers fabricate calls to these five functions even if
you don=E2=80=99t call them yourself, which is how they were selected. (Not
because I like them. <a href=3D"https://nullprogram.com/blog/2023/02/11/">I=
 really don=E2=80=99t.</a>). If a <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlig=
hter-rouge">-nostdlib</code> build
complains about these, too, then add <code class=3D"language-plaintext high=
lighter-rouge">-lmemory</code>.</p>

<div class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight=
"><pre class=3D"highlight"><code>$ gcc -nostdlib ... -lchkstk -lmemory
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>In MSVC the equivalent option is <code class=3D"language-plaintext highl=
ighter-rouge">/nodefaultlib</code>, after which you may see
missing chkstk errors, and perhaps more. <code class=3D"language-plaintext =
highlighter-rouge">libchkstk.a</code> is compatible with
MSVC, and <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">link.exe</co=
de> doesn=E2=80=99t care that the extension is <code class=3D"language-plai=
ntext highlighter-rouge">.a</code> rather than
<code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">.lib</code>, so supply=
 it at link time. Same goes for <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlight=
er-rouge">libmemory.a</code> if you need
any of those, too.</p>

<div class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight=
"><pre class=3D"highlight"><code>$ cl ... /link /nodefaultlib libchkstk.a l=
ibmemory.a
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>While I despise licenses, I still take them seriously in the software I
distribute. With libchkstk I have another tool to get it under control.</p>

